<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

''Verdict: HOLD.'' The committed code leaves custody and isolation failures in the paths below. This was a cold, read-only source review; I did not run tests. ''MEASURED'' identifies code directly observed; ''INFER'' identifies the consequence derived from it.

* ''P1 — Production sessions do not enforce per-face custody.'' ''MEASURED:'' `packages/lararium-web/src/workers/vessel-host.ts:257` opens every session without a face; `:172` supplies no provider door, and `:215` binds fleet-join custody through the browser-wide tree. The design document explicitly names the unmigrated rows and unwired provider as residuals. ''INFER:'' Production requests operate through the shared vessel and keystore rather than the face-scoped admission, booking, sealing and row paths. The claimed K15 isolation and one-provider-per-face behavior are therefore not established at the product door.

* ''P1 — Closing a session does not stop underlying custody work.'' ''MEASURED:'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/vessel-sessions.ts:284` rejects a wrapper promise but does not cancel or await its body. A face unlock or first bind can continue after that rejection and seat its FCK at `browser-face-custody.ts:332` or `:308`; an asking session’s drop does not run the admitted-session lock (`vessel-sessions.ts:319`). Face row operations also copy the FCK before asynchronous I/O (`browser-face-custody.ts:213`). ''INFER:'' Closing during unlock can leave a newly seated key after the last session has gone; a pending row operation can retain a key copy and complete after closure. The same wrapper does not fence other in-flight work.

* ''P1 — A tampered index can make one face’s clear delete a sibling.'' ''MEASURED:'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-face-custody.ts:368` routes clear through the unauthenticated index, proves only that the supplied credential opens the routed tree, then deletes its rows and tree at `:379`. Unlike unlock at `:326`, clear does not authenticate the sealed anchor against the requested face. ''INFER:'' If A’s index entry points to B’s tree and the supplied route opens B—such as a shared passphrase—confirming clear for A deletes B’s custody.

* ''P2 — A failed switch can leave the target FCK seated.'' ''MEASURED:'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/vessel-sessions.ts:543` preflights by unlocking and seating the target; if the subsequent intent hook fails, `:558` drops the session while it still wears the old face. ''INFER:'' With another session keeping the house alive, the target key remains held without an admitted target session. Cleanup must account for preflight keys on every failed or interrupted switch.

The diff documents real-device lifecycle checks and production row migration as outstanding; this review does not establish those properties.

